Privately Attack Chains =E2=80=94 defensive synthesis

1 NEW ATTACK CHAINS

AC-2026-NPM-CLOUD-PIVOT =E2=80=94 ACTIVE/RECENT. Fingerprint: typosquatted =
npm
package -> lifecycle-hook execution -> host/CI credential harvest ->
cloud/Vault/npm authority -> downstream supply-chain pivot. CONFIRMED:
Microsoft documented 14 malicious npm packages using install-time execution
to harvest AWS credentials, HashiCorp Vault tokens, GitHub Actions/CI
secrets and npm publish tokens; the publish tokens enable follow-on
supply-chain pivots. UNKNOWN: public evidence does not establish a specific
successful downstream victim compromise from those stolen tokens. Trust
boundaries: registry->developer/CI; CI identity->cloud/Vault; package
consumer->package publisher. Detection: new lifecycle hooks, unexpected
Bun/runtime downloads, IMDS/ECS credential access from package-install
processes, registry-token access during install, outbound traffic from
package-manager child processes. Containment: disable install scripts where
feasible, ephemeral CI credentials, workload identity rather than static
cloud keys, registry publishing with short-lived/provenance-bound authority=
.

AC-2026-TRIVY-RESIDUAL-AUTHORITY =E2=80=94 RECENT. Fingerprint: privileged =
CI
workflow flaw -> PAT theft -> repository authority -> incomplete credential
rotation/residual service-account authority -> force-moved trusted
tags/release pipeline -> downstream CI execution -> secret harvesting.
CONFIRMED: public reporting on the Trivy incidents documents an initial
pull_request_target-related PAT compromise, repository takeover,
incomplete/non-atomic rotation leaving residual access, later authenticated
tag manipulation, release propagation and credential-stealing payloads in
CI. CONFIRMED downstream execution capability; UNKNOWN at this evidence
tier whether any particular downstream organization suffered a further
cloud/service compromise. This chain is especially important because the
second incident depends on authority surviving remediation rather than a
new exploit. Detection: privileged PR-target workflows, tag target changes,
release commits absent normal review ancestry, service-account use after
rotation events, secret reads/network egress from security-tool steps.
Containment: atomic credential invalidation across human/bot/service
identities; immutable SHA pins; protected release tags; separate release
signing authority; post-incident inventory of all delegated credentials.

2 MATERIAL CHAIN UPDATES
No prior Attack Chains state was found in Gmail Sent, so no canonical
prior-chain delta could be safely asserted. Latest Scanner state was
recovered; Incident Intake and Repo Forensics reports were not present in
Sent search, so this run does not invent cross-agent incident IDs.

3 TOP RECURRING SECOND-ORDER MECHANISMS
A. Package install execution -> broad developer/CI secret surface ->
package/cloud authority.
B. CI/repository credential -> trusted tag/release mutation -> downstream
execution.
C. Incomplete remediation -> residual bot/service authority -> second
compromise without a new entry exploit.
D. Security/developer tooling as a privileged execution surface: tools
naturally run where secrets and release authority coexist.

4 STAGE/EDGE GRAPHS
AC-2026-NPM-CLOUD-PIVOT: registry package [CONFIRMED] -> lifecycle
execution [CONFIRMED] -> AWS/Vault/CI/npm credential access [CONFIRMED] ->
cloud/registry authority [CONFIRMED capability] -> downstream malicious
publish [CONFIRMED capability; successful external victim pivot UNKNOWN].
Persistence UNKNOWN.
AC-2026-TRIVY-RESIDUAL-AUTHORITY: pull_request_target weakness [CONFIRMED]
-> PAT theft [CONFIRMED] -> repo control [CONFIRMED] -> incomplete
rotation/residual authority [CONFIRMED] -> trusted tags/releases modified
[CONFIRMED] -> downstream CI execution [CONFIRMED] -> secret
collection/exfil capability [CONFIRMED] -> further victim-side privileged
action [UNKNOWN].

5 UNKNOWN LINKS WORTH RESOLVING
Highest information gain: whether public primary evidence confirms actual
downstream cloud/registry actions after credential theft; exact residual
credential class used in the second Trivy compromise; whether any Scanner
objects map to these canonical mechanisms; whether current agent-tool
incidents demonstrate persistence through memory/session rather than
conventional CI/package state.

6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION PRIMITIVES
Authority boundaries are the choke point: package install should not
inherit release/cloud authority; PR evaluation should not inherit
repository secrets; release identities should be separate from build
identities; rotation must enumerate human, bot, app, deploy-key, registry
and cloud credentials as one revocation transaction. Useful primitives:
process-tree telemetry for package-manager hooks; IMDS/Vault/secret-store
access by unexpected build children; tag-ref mutation alerts; release
provenance mismatch; outbound egress from security-tool steps;
service-account activity after incident revocation timestamp.

7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE
LAB-1: disposable npm registry/package with a benign preinstall script
reading only fake environment variables; verify CI alerts on secret-access
pattern and network-deny policy. LAB-2: disposable GitHub repo with dummy
token and protected/unprotected tags; simulate token rotation and verify
every bot/app/deploy credential is invalidated. LAB-3: fake release
pipeline with immutable SHA pinning versus mutable tag; demonstrate
detection of tag retargeting without external targets. No real credentials,
wallets or third-party infrastructure.

8 CROSS-CASE CLUSTERS
CLUSTER-CI-AUTHORITY-CASCADE: Trivy + npm cloud-secret campaigns share the
transition developer/CI execution -> credential material -> broader
authority. CLUSTER-RESIDUAL-AUTHORITY: Trivy uniquely supplies strong
public evidence that incomplete credential rotation can bridge incident 1
to incident 2. CLUSTER-REGISTRY-PROPAGATION: npm publish-token theft
establishes a mechanism for consumer->maintainer->consumer propagation,
while specific downstream success remains case-dependent.

